1. Architecture credit and adapted portions: cloudflare/security-audit-skill (MIT)

   The overall shape of this skill's multi-agent audit follows the design of
   the security-audit skill published by Cloudflare at
   https://github.com/cloudflare/security-audit-skill under the MIT License:
   a phased run that starts with reconnaissance, a coverage ledger that
   records every unit of work and its state, hunter agents that work through
   that ledger, coverage critics that look for gaps, independent verification
   of every candidate before it becomes a finding, and schema validators that
   check the files the run writes.

   ui-audit audits how a web application works and looks, not its security,
   and its instructions were written for that purpose. Some parts are adapted
   from security-audit-skill rather than written from scratch:

   - the findings schema's field structure (trace, evidence, conditions,
     execution, remediation, confidence) in report-schema.json;
   - a few input-hygiene patterns in the validators (visible-content,
     reserved Windows path component and unsafe diagnostic character checks,
     and the bounded walk over object fields);
   - a few section-heading conventions in the companion and hunting files.

   Because those portions come from a project published under the MIT
   License, its copyright and permission notice is reproduced in section 5.


2. Pixel comparison algorithm (published description, reimplemented)

   scripts/ui-diff.mjs compares screenshots with two published techniques:

   - a perceptual colour difference measured in the YIQ colour space, after
     Y. Kotsarenko and F. Ramos, "Measuring perceived color difference using
     YIQ NTSC transmission color space in mobile applications" (2010);
   - an anti-aliased pixel detector, after V. Vysniauskas, "Anti-aliased
     pixel and intensity slope detector" (2009).

   Both techniques are widely known through the pixelmatch library
   (https://github.com/mapbox/pixelmatch, ISC License), which Playwright also
   bundles. ui-audit reimplements them from their published descriptions in
   its own code. No source code from pixelmatch or from Playwright was copied.


3. Third-party software

   playwright-core (Apache License 2.0)
     https://github.com/microsoft/playwright
     The only npm dependency, pinned to version 1.63.0 in package.json and
     installed by "npm ci" into this skill's own node_modules folder. It is a
     dependency and is not vendored: no Playwright file is part of this
     repository.

   axe-core (Mozilla Public License 2.0)
     https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core
     Not distributed with this skill and never downloaded by it. When the
     audited project already has axe-core installed (for example as a
     dependency of eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y or Lighthouse), the browser probe
     can load that copy, read-only, from the project's own node_modules
     folder. If the project has no copy, the probe skips axe and reports the
     gap. Any future decision to vendor axe-core would have to ship it with
     its MPL-2.0 licence text and header.


4. Standards and research

   Thresholds and rules in this skill cite public documents (W3C WCAG 2.2,
   web.dev, Chrome for Developers, framework documentation, Nielsen Norman
   Group, Baymard Institute, platform design guidelines and others). They are
   listed with links in SOURCES.md. The skill restates them in its own words;
   it does not reproduce those documents.


